<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Harry Nkumbula</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1916">1916</a>–<a href="/years/1983">1983</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Harry Nkumbula was a Zambian nationalist who led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-rhodesia">Northern Rhodesia</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-national-congress">African National Congress</a> from 1951 and campaigned against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federation-of-rhodesia-and-nyasaland">Federation of Rhodesia and Nyasaland</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenneth-kaunda">Kenneth Kaunda</a> and other younger leaders broke away from him in 1958, and their movement soon became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-national-independence-party">United National Independence Party</a>. Nkumbula's congress joined a coalition government in 1962, and he remained the main opposition leader until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a> became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-party-state">one-party state</a> in 1972.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/harry-nkumbula.jpg" alt="Harry Nkumbula" width="228" height="256" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Times of Zambia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Harry_Nkumbula.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Harry Mwaanga Nkumbula was born on 15 January 1916 in the village of Maala in the Namwala district of the Southern Province, the youngest of three children and the only son. He was educated at Methodist mission schools, completed Standard VI at the Kafue Training Institute in 1934 and taught in Namwala District. In 1938 he joined the government teaching service and later worked at Kitwe and Mufulira on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copperbelt">Copperbelt</a>, where he was secretary of the Mufulira Welfare Association and a co-founder of the Kitwe African Society.</p><p>With the support of the settler politician Sir Stewart Gore-Browne he went in 1946 to Makerere University College in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kampala">Kampala</a>, and then took a diploma at the Institute of Education of the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. In 1949 he worked with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hastings-banda">Hastings Kamuzu Banda</a> of Nyasaland on a document setting out African opposition to the proposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federation-of-rhodesia-and-nyasaland">Central African Federation</a>. He failed his examinations in economics at the London School of Economics and returned home without a degree early in 1950.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Elected president of the Northern Rhodesian African Congress in 1951, he called a national strike against federation, presented as a national day of prayer, but the African Mineworkers' Union under Lawrence Katilungu campaigned against it and it failed. In 1954 he and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenneth-kaunda">Kenneth Kaunda</a>, the congress's secretary general from 1953, organised a partly successful boycott of European-owned butcheries in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lusaka">Lusaka</a>. In early 1955 the two men were jailed together for two months with hard labour for distributing literature the authorities called subversive.</p><p>He spent April 1961 to January 1962 in prison for causing death by dangerous driving. Before the elections of October 1962 he accepted funding from Moise Tshombe's regime in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/katanga">Katanga</a> and made a secret pact with the United Federal Party. His congress won seven seats and the balance of power; he chose a coalition with Kaunda's United National Independence Party and became minister of African education. After the elections of January 1964, in which his party won ten seats to UNIP's fifty-five, he became leader of the opposition.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 27 June 1973 he signed the Choma Declaration and joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-national-independence-party">UNIP</a>, and his party ceased to exist when parliament was dissolved that October. Claims that Kaunda bought him off with an emerald mine are doubted, as the mine did not come into his possession until 1975. In 1978 he and Simon Kapwepwe tried to stand against Kaunda for the presidential nomination and were outmanoeuvred. He died on 8 October 1983, aged 67.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Harry Nkumbula” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its leaders included two former teachers, <b>Harry Nkumbula</b> and Kenneth Kaunda.</blockquote><a href="/empire/northern-rhodesia">Northern Rhodesia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Opposition led by <b>Harry Nkumbula</b> and Kenneth Kaunda's United National Independence Party ended the federation, and Northern Rhodesia became independent as Zambia on 24 October 1964 with Kaunda as president.</blockquote><a href="/state/northern-rhodesia">Northern Rhodesia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Namwala District, in what is now <a href="/countries/zambia">Zambia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lusaka">Lusaka</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/zambia">Zambia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-15.91,26.77&amp;year=1959" aria-label="Namwala District on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5280 2755 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Harry Nkumbula"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5730 3025h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5730 3025h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-15.91,26.77&amp;year=1959">What was here in 1959? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
